{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide an encrypted volume mounted read-write at /srv/secure on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After the final reboot, /srv/secure was directly observed as a read-write ext4 mount backed by /dev/mapper/secure. cryptsetup reported an active LUKS2 AES-XTS mapping in read/write mode, and a post-reboot write/read test succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ad20984c2e4a4a66"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The encrypted volume must unlock and mount automatically at boot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded reboot test showed a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged reboot, changed boot identity, and full recovery. After boot, both OpenRC services were enabled and started, the LUKS mapping was active, /srv/secure was mounted read-write, prior data persisted, and a new write succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9756e3f6192843ee","cmd-622546f26d8243c0","cmd-df8b185bfd19404b","cmd-ad20984c2e4a4a66","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"The mutations were closely connected to installing, configuring, testing, correcting, and verifying the encrypted volume. The initially faulty boot arrangement was bounded and then cleanly stopped and replaced after it produced a read-only mount; this corrective sequence contributed directly to the final working design. The only unnecessary retained residue was two reboot log files under /tmp, created by cmd-12ec7556fa114ac5 and cmd-622546f26d8243c0 and not removed. No new listening service, package inconsistency, or weakened security control was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-12ec7556fa114ac5","cmd-622546f26d8243c0","cmd-bba3bb6cf14e4ad1","cmd-bbcc3e1eb1624cf2","cmd-8ad76abb2cd24416","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. node1 has a functioning LUKS2-backed ext4 volume at /srv/secure, and direct reboot-transition evidence demonstrates automatic unlock, automatic mounting, data persistence, and post-reboot write capability. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and independently corroborated by the lifecycle boot-identity observation.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector's generic failed-service probe is systemd-specific and unsupported on Alpine; service health is instead established directly with OpenRC commands.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory the task-specific files and configuration, so their final state is supported primarily by executor command evidence."]}
